Post-Viral Fatigue Syndrome
Idiopathic & Benign Entities
Profound fatigue following a viral infection, Post-exertional malaise, Unrefreshing sleep/brain fog, Normal baseline inflammatory markers

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: IBE-039
Reason for Visit: Acute functional collapse, severe orthostatic intolerance, and profound exhaustion mimicking acute cardiopulmonary failure.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and spouse
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified a patient experiencing a sudden, severe physiological crash resulting in an inability to stand, profound lethargy, and palpitations. Urgent after-hours assessment was required to definitively perform a cardiopulmonary examination, exclude acute myocarditis, pulmonary embolism, or secondary bacterial sepsis, and safely manage the acute physical collapse prior to the next available in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "I tried to vacuum the house today, and my body completely shut down; my heart is racing, my head is pounding, and I can't even lift my arms."
History of Present Illness: Patient presents with profound, unrelenting generalized fatigue that has lingered for 6 weeks explicitly following a documented acute viral infection (Influenza A). Today, the patient is experiencing severe post-exertional malaise (PEM), characterized by a disproportionate physical crash following strictly minor physical exertion (vacuuming). The patient reports unrefreshing sleep, severe cognitive 'brain fog', and migrating muscle pain. Current acute symptoms include exertional tachycardia and profound orthostatic intolerance upon standing, leading to near-syncope. There is a complete absence of ongoing fever, purulent cough, or shortness of breath at rest.
Functional Status: Bedbound strictly due to profound exhaustion and severe orthostatic dizziness.
Social/Safety Concerns: Inability to perform basic ADLs safely; relying entirely on spouse for hydration and care.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 105/65 | HR: 115 | Temp: 36.6 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 70 Kg
General: Pale, exhausted, lying completely flat, speaking very softly.
Physical Exam: Cardiovascular exam reveals a regular tachycardia without murmurs or gallops; HR returns to 80 bpm when strictly supine for 10 minutes, indicating marked orthostatic tachycardia. Respiratory exam reveals perfectly clear lungs with excellent air entry bilaterally. Neurological exam is grossly intact without focal deficits. No cervical lymphadenopathy. Complete absence of objective joint swelling. Documented history of completely normal baseline inflammatory markers (CRP <5), full blood count, and normal renal/hepatic function tests 2 weeks ago confirming resolution of the acute virus.
Environmental Observations: Vacuum cleaner abandoned in the middle of the hallway.
Medication Reconciliation: Nil regular medications; taking simple analgesics for migrating muscle aches.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Post-Viral Fatigue Syndrome - Status: Acute post-exertional crash
Differentials:

Acute Viral Myocarditis - Ruled out by normal SpO2, clear lungs (no heart failure signs), absence of chest pain, and isolated orthostatic (rather than continuous resting) tachycardia.

Secondary Bacterial Infection (e.g., Pneumonia) - Ruled out explicitly by the strict absence of fever, completely clear lungs, and normal respiratory rate.

Major Depressive Disorder - Unlikely as the primary cause given the distinct, severe orthostatic tachycardia and the classic physiological PEM crash directly following physical exertion.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient required an urgent after-hours home visit due to a severe, acute functional collapse and pronounced orthostatic tachycardia mimicking a serious cardiopulmonary complication (e.g., myocarditis or pulmonary embolism) following a recent viral illness. The profound inability to safely stand necessitated an immediate, hands-on clinical examination. By definitively confirming normal pulmonary function, ruling out resting cardiopulmonary distress, and diagnosing a classic post-exertional dysautonomic crash, the physician safely initiated immediate bed-based hydration protocols. This definitive clinical exclusion prevented a highly stressful and completely unnecessary emergency department admission, satisfying the criteria for MBS Item 597.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribed oral rehydration solution (ORS) and advised aggressive, strict horizontal rest for the next 48 hours. Advised against attempting to 'push through' the fatigue.
Positive Behavioral Change: When attempting to increase daily activity, establish a rigid baseline that is strictly 50% of your perceived available energy, and stop activity immediately when that limit is reached. By intentionally stopping long before physical exhaustion hits, you prevent the severe physiological penalty of a crash, directly rewarding the behavior with stabilized, predictable baseline energy levels over the coming weeks.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): Shower chair.
Follow-up: Routine GP follow-up via telehealth in 1 week to monitor crash recovery and discuss pacing strategies.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ED presentation or call 000 if developing acute central chest pain, severe shortness of breath at rest, witnessed loss of consciousness (fainting), or a return of a high fever.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis assumes previously normal blood work remains stable; if new cardiopulmonary symptoms develop at rest, immediate reassessment is required.
Clinical Course: Post-viral fatigue is a genuine physiological state where your cells' energy production and autonomic nervous system are temporarily damaged by the virus. Recovery is slow and requires aggressive, disciplined pacing, but gradual improvement is the expected outcome.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek immediate emergency medical attention if you develop heavy chest pain, if you struggle to breathe while lying completely still, or if you actually pass out.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Your body currently lacks the metabolic capacity to recover normally from exertion. The racing heart when you stand is because your nervous system is struggling to regulate blood pressure after the virus. Aggressive horizontal rest and high fluid intake are physically required to reset your baseline.
Actionable Steps: Keep a bottle of electrolyte fluid immediately next to your bed and take frequent small sips while lying completely flat. Achieving basic hydration without triggering orthostatic stress will lower your heart rate, immediately reinforcing the physiological benefit of resting horizontally.

